WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:11.290
- Hello everyone, I am Priya Natarajan.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:14.510
I am the current Director
of the Franke Program

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:17.010
in Science and the Humanities at Yale

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:19.380
and I'm an astrophysicist.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.560
I am absolutely delighted
that you are all here

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.090
for the first convening of the year.

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:29.520
So, here's wishing everyone a
very happy and healthy 2021.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.500
I want to mention that it
is hard not to celebrate

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.430
the recent shattering of a glass ceiling

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:38.490
that we all witnessed yesterday.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.630
The swearing end of the
first female vice president

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:43.600
who is black and South Asian.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.430
So, I just wanted to start

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:49.610
off by saying that despite
the pandemic and everything,

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:53.380
we don't know why this took so
long but we are so delighted

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:56.220
and proud to have this happen.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.350
First, I would like to recognize

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.910
and thank Richard and Barbara Franke

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:04.800
who are here with us today
for the generous support

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.800
of the Franke Program
and many other events

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:12.800
and programs at Yale and we
are really happy to have you

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:16.923
participate so actively in
our programming as well.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.610
So, before I tell you
about the exciting talk

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:24.020
we have in store today,
I wanted to remind you

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:27.180
that we are recording this event

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:31.030
and therefore all participants
must mute their videos.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:34.590
And as for submitting questions,

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:38.920
please start sending them
as per usual to the chat.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.640
And we have a slightly
altered format today

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.310
and I will mention that briefly.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:46.790
So, we'll start off with the talk,

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:51.790
followed by sort of a brief
discussion and then the Q and A.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.290
So today's talk, as you all know

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:58.300
is part of the ongoing
"Mapping as Knowing Series"

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.640
that we commenced a couple of years ago.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.340
It was of course partly driven
by my own personal obsession

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.140
with mapping and cartography

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:08.920
but it turns out, it's a very,
very rich intellectual tool.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:11.470
Mapping has been a very
important intellectual tool

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.120
to study more than just
mapping physical terrain.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.190
The history of cartography is of course,

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:21.980
as we all know, very
deeply tied to the voyages

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:25.200
of exploration and colonial conquest.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.350
However, these very tools
that created those maps

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:32.290
of domination can be used
today to understand better

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:34.750
the legacy of segregation and inequality

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:36.860
that they have left behind.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:40.020
Our speaker today is an exemplary artist

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.310
who has managed just that.

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:46.080
Use maps to eliminate
geographies of segregation

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.890
to raise awareness of how these
divisions are deeply rooted

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:54.730
in our society, culture
and our daily lives

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:58.970
of where and they inform where we live.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:01.300
Tonika Johnson, our speaker today

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:04.030
is a photographer social justice artists

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:06.940
and lifelong resident
of Chicago South side,

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:08.660
neighborhood of Englewood.

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:12.100
She's also co-founder of two
community-based organizations,

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.860
Englewood Arts Collective

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.690
and Residents Association
of Greater Englewood

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:20.180
which seek to reframe the
narrative of Southside communities

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:23.660
and mobilize people and
resources for positive change.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:25.150
Within her artistic practice,

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:28.210
Tonika often explores urban
segregation and documents,

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:31.160
the nuance and richness
of the black community

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.820
countering pervasive media depictions

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:36.220
of Chicago's violence and crime.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:39.000
As a trained photo journalist
and a former teaching artists,

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.550
she's been engaged in
building an artistic legacy

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:45.750
that has gained enormous
recognition in the last four years.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:49.280
In 2017, she was recognized
by Chicago magazine

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.620
as a Chicago Award of the
Year for her photography

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:53.790
of Englewood's everyday beauty.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.840
Her Englewood based photography projects

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:59.370
from the inside and everyday
rituals were exhibited

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:01.110
at Rootwork Gallery in Pilsen,

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:02.460
the Chicago Cultural Center,

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.610
the Harold Washington Library Center

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:08.010
and at Loyola University's Museum of Art.

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:09.840
Tonika is gonna be talking to us today

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.430
about her Folded Map Project
which visually investigates

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:15.500
the disparities among Chicago residents

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:19.130
who are map twins living
on opposite ends of streets

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:22.080
that span the city's
racial and economic divides

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:24.673
and brings them together
to have a conversation.

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:29.350
This project was also
exhibited at LUMA in 2018.

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:32.740
LUMA is a Loyola
University's Museum of Art.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:35.840
Now, an excerpt of this
project is currently on display

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.010
at the museum of contemporary art

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:40.650
as part of the long dream exhibition.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:44.800
Since 2018, Tonika has
transformed Folded Map

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.110
into an advocacy and
policy influencing tool

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:51.620
that invites audiences to
open a dialogue and question

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:53.690
how we are all socially impacted

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:55.960
by racial and institutional conditions

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.210
that segregate the city.

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:00.200
In 2020, she formalized
the Folded Map Project

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.270
into a nonprofit organization

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:05.060
where she serves as
the executive director.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:07.730
She has won many awards and accolades,

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:08.680
I'll just name a few.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:11.010
She was named one of
Field Foundations leaders

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.260
for New Chicago and was appointed

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:15.430
as a member of the
Cultural Advisory Council

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:17.660
for the Department of Cultural Affairs

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:20.293
and special events by
the Chicago City Council.

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:24.110
I'm delighted that after our talk,

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:28.640
we will follow it up with a
brief conversation with our own

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:32.530
resident cartographer, Bill
Rankin who is a faculty

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:36.220
in the Departments of the
History of Science and Medicine

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:40.040
who has worked extensively on
various uses of cartography

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:43.730
and whose most exciting
recent article was titled

00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:44.750
and it's very relevant

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:48.670
to what what Tonika will
be telling us today.

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:51.440
It's titled "Race and the
Territorial Imaginary:

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:53.900
Reckoning with the Demographic Cartography

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:55.540
of the United States."

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:59.130
Following this, we open up for Q and A

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:04.130
and I just wanted to mention and thank Ty

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.280
for bringing Tonika and
the Folded Map Project

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:10.320
to my attention and to Tonika
for so graciously agreeing

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:12.340
to talk so promptly.

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.780
And we wanted to open the
series this year with her

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:16.240
so thank you so much Tonika

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:20.160
for being here and talking today.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:23.840
Her work really exemplifies
this cross section

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:27.940
of what the between the sciences
and the humanities can do.

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:32.300
How the tools from science
and technology can be used

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:36.150
to understand and produce
deeper cultural meaning

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:39.190
and interrogate our
society and its values.

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:43.573
So, Tonika without much
further ado, here's onto you.

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:48.300
- Hi, thank you so much

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:51.720
for that amazing introduction and welcome.

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.470
I'm so delighted to be here with you all

00:06:54.470 --> 00:06:59.050
and excited for you all to
view the Folded Map film

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:02.870
that I did create during the pandemic

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:05.240
so that I would be able
to have an opportunity

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:10.240
to engage with others
despite not being able to do

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:11.740
in-person presentation.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:15.650
So, I'm really glad to
be here with you all

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:19.040
and excited to have the
conversation with you

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:22.460
after you view the Folded Map film.

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:25.714
- Great, thanks very
much, so I will go ahead

00:07:25.714 --> 00:07:27.510
and Tonika, may I start?

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:28.343
- Yes.

00:07:31.364 --> 00:07:33.947
(air whooshes)

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:39.803
I'm from South side, Chicago
Englewood neighborhood.

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:42.570
I'm sure the words you've heard

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:46.780
to describe Englewood include black,

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:50.293
dangerous, poor, gun violence.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:55.573
However, Englewood is where
I grew up and still live.

00:07:56.890 --> 00:07:59.230
Before I tell you about Folded Map,

00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:02.500
I'd like you to think
about how you came to live

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:04.450
in the neighborhood that you grew up in

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:10.190
or where you live now, what
influenced your decision?

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:11.313
Who did you talk to?

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:14.800
And while you're thinking about that,

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:17.750
I'm gonna tell you how I
came to live in Englewood

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:21.493
and how I created the Folded Map Project.

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:27.513
It all started with my
grandmother, Marilyn G Tennie.

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:32.840
She came to Chicago
from down South in 1962

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:36.193
on the tail end of the Great Migration.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:41.190
She wanted a better life, to
have a professional career

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:46.110
and she achieved it
when she came to Chicago

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:50.440
and got a job as an
administrative assistant

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:52.633
at the local Social Security office.

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:56.747
What black folks would call
'a good government job.'

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:04.010
For seven years, she saved her money

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:06.980
while working at the
Social Security office

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:11.410
to purchase a beautiful
two-flat brick building

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:13.353
on 62nd and Loomis in Englewood.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:17.900
The building that I grew up in with my mom

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:19.663
and my two uncles.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:23.430
My childhood was beautiful

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:28.313
unlike anything people
would imagine in Englewood.

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:34.770
I played outside every day, I
rode my bike with my friends,

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:39.363
I met my first friends in life,
one of them named Raymond.

00:09:40.250 --> 00:09:42.540
Him and I would go to the
corner store every day

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:45.053
for cookies, pickles and chips.

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:49.500
Even my grandmother's
best friend, Ms. Patterson

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:50.883
lived right next door.

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:53.823
She had the same migration story.

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:58.540
Every summer, Ms. Patterson
would make homemade ice cream

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:00.873
and sell it to the kids for 25 cents.

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:07.572
It was also on this block
that I started high school.

00:10:07.572 --> 00:10:09.400
(upbeat music)

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:13.314
I started going to Lane Tech High School.

00:10:13.314 --> 00:10:15.030
And for those that don't know,

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:17.460
it's a selective enrollment high school,

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:20.550
15 miles north of Englewood.

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:25.550
And it's very diverse with a
student body of 4,000 students.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:32.510
But everyday, I would have to
be at the bus stop by 5:45 AM

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:37.023
just to make it to
school by eight o'clock.

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:43.670
And on that every day commute,
I would look out the window

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:46.580
listening to my Walkman

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:51.900
and notice how my neighborhood
was very different

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:55.467
from the neighborhood that
my high school was in.

00:10:55.467 --> 00:11:00.467
And this was in 1993, so there
was no GPS, no cell phones.

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:03.650
If you want it to go anywhere,

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:07.043
you had to know the address,
you had to know the streets.

00:11:08.098 --> 00:11:10.663
And so, as I was on the
train going to school,

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:16.580
I would notice that my
neighborhood had vacant lots

00:11:18.790 --> 00:11:22.613
and the neighborhood and Lane
Tech was in didn't have any.

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:28.540
My neighborhood had hundreds
of storefront churches

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:31.720
and there was none

00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:33.820
in the neighborhood my high school was in.

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:38.130
My neighborhood had so
many beauty supplies stores

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:41.273
and I didn't see any up North.

00:11:43.453 --> 00:11:46.463
And I would always wonder, "Why is that?"

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:53.000
And there's also one detail
that I paid extra attention to.

00:11:55.430 --> 00:11:56.263
The streets.

00:11:57.460 --> 00:12:01.360
I noticed that the streets were
named the same in Englewood

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:03.710
and also 15 miles North

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:06.160
in the neighborhood that
my high school was in.

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:11.160
Streets like Ashland,
Paulina, Wolcott and Western.

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:16.343
And even though those
streets named the same,

00:12:17.290 --> 00:12:18.993
they looked completely different.

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:34.200
Ultimately, I want Folded
Map to help us heal

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:36.380
and get to know each other

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:42.300
so that we can tear down the
racist walls that divide us.

00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:47.063
For more information, go
to foldedmapproject.com.

00:12:48.221 --> 00:12:52.156
This is Tonika Johnson,
for the Folded Map Project.

00:12:52.156 --> 00:12:54.739
(upbeat music)

00:12:56.960 --> 00:13:01.627
- Okay, Tonika that was
brilliant and I think we can see

00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:07.020
that the point that you are trying to make

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:12.010
is extremely powerful about how
we deal with systemic racism

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:16.980
is not by theorizing about
it but really look deeper

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:20.260
into our own personal
lives and lived experience.

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:21.611
And with something as ostensibly simple

00:13:21.611 --> 00:13:26.540
as what you've shown us just addresses

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:31.540
that our twinned see how
different life experiences are

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:34.360
and the history of how that came to be.

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:37.470
I'm sure the city of New
Haven would be a place

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:42.470
that it would be fabulous to
have your project come to.

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:47.267
So, I wanted to share the
link that you can all...

00:13:52.171 --> 00:13:53.310
Tonika, do you wanna tell us

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:56.743
what people can download from the website?

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:02.720
- Yes, so, Priya you'll share the link.

00:14:03.670 --> 00:14:05.360
- Yeah, I just-
- Okay, perfect.

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:07.900
So, the link that Priya
is sharing with you

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:12.810
is a link to the latest
expansion of Folded Map

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:15.423
which is the Folded Map action kit.

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:20.450
The contact list for Folded
Map over the past three years

00:14:20.450 --> 00:14:23.340
has grown to include 800 people

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:26.020
who all wanted to meet their map twins.

00:14:26.020 --> 00:14:28.370
And since I knew I wasn't going to be able

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:31.783
to actually facilitate
all of those meetings,

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:36.000
it allowed me to create a way for people

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:39.480
to have their own self guided experience

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:43.580
as close as possible to kind
of walking through the shoes

00:14:43.580 --> 00:14:46.577
of a resident in their
map twin neighborhood.

00:14:46.577 --> 00:14:49.620
And so, the action kit
includes information

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:53.710
about the project as
well as the participants,

00:14:53.710 --> 00:14:57.530
neighborhoods, so you can see
the statistical difference

00:14:57.530 --> 00:15:02.220
from economics, education,
business corridor, housing

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:05.110
and then it also invites you to describe

00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:08.870
your own neighborhood to answer
the questions of Folded Map

00:15:08.870 --> 00:15:11.720
and then to actually go run errands

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:13.700
in your map twin neighborhoods.

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:17.040
So, the errands is an invitation

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:20.770
one to go visit another
neighborhood but also an opportunity

00:15:20.770 --> 00:15:24.370
to kind of disrupt the stereotypes

00:15:24.370 --> 00:15:28.190
that have been embedded in Chicago

00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:30.370
and heads of other neighborhoods.

00:15:30.370 --> 00:15:32.730
So in order to do that,

00:15:32.730 --> 00:15:36.930
I figured that just go run
errands in another neighborhood.

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:40.380
You'll really be able
to not only meet people

00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:43.640
in that neighborhood or who
are doing the exact same thing,

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:46.310
basic things that we all do

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:48.210
but you'll also be able to understand

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:52.150
how different it is and
what disparity looks like.

00:15:52.150 --> 00:15:55.780
How convenient it is, or
how inconvenient that it is.

00:15:55.780 --> 00:16:00.780
So, in addition to instructing
people to do these errands,

00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:04.320
I'm also inviting them to
share back their experience

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:07.060
in the Folded Map Action Kit workbook,

00:16:07.060 --> 00:16:08.900
which is a very simple workbook.

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:11.580
We just want you to
share what you thought.

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:13.530
How was the experience?

00:16:13.530 --> 00:16:17.970
And you can upload that
on to Folded Maps website.

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:21.030
So, we have the link that Priya sent

00:16:21.030 --> 00:16:23.810
