Englewood's home ownership

00:24:58.480 --> 00:25:00.460
rate ever higher?

00:25:00.460 --> 00:25:03.640
because it's currently like 23%.

00:25:03.640 --> 00:25:08.640
And trying to find the
answer to that question

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:13.750
as well as meeting amazing
people doing wonderful research

00:25:15.010 --> 00:25:19.070
led me to the information
about land sale contracts

00:25:19.070 --> 00:25:21.540
specifically in Greater Englewood.

00:25:21.540 --> 00:25:25.370
So, that is what my next
project will be focusing on.

00:25:25.370 --> 00:25:27.880
My goal is to make landmarkers

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:32.070
for the homes that are still
existing in Greater Englewood

00:25:32.070 --> 00:25:34.500
that was sold land sale contracts

00:25:34.500 --> 00:25:39.500
and of course, most of them
are vacant or demolished

00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:42.120
so it's a direct connection

00:25:42.120 --> 00:25:45.693
to that kind of discriminatory practice.

00:25:50.858 --> 00:25:54.430
- I'm sharing another map here.

00:25:54.430 --> 00:25:56.189
I did this for a local newspaper

00:25:56.189 --> 00:25:58.940
of New Haven independent number years ago

00:26:00.420 --> 00:26:01.920
and there was a local nonprofit here

00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:04.280
who had a bunch of really
good data about foreclosures

00:26:04.280 --> 00:26:09.280
in New Haven and we did this
map to kind of push back

00:26:09.790 --> 00:26:11.770
against some of the narratives happening

00:26:11.770 --> 00:26:14.070
with the foreclosure crisis
that we're highlighting

00:26:14.070 --> 00:26:17.600
and centering the stories of
of white middle class families.

00:26:17.600 --> 00:26:20.070
And of course there,
their stories were serious

00:26:20.070 --> 00:26:24.480
and for sure but the vast
majority of actual foreclosures

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:28.170
were happening in communities of color.

00:26:28.170 --> 00:26:32.180
And so, I completely agree
that the maps of segregation

00:26:32.180 --> 00:26:33.237
and racial segregation

00:26:33.237 --> 00:26:36.763
and the maps of housing
inequality are the same map.

00:26:38.180 --> 00:26:42.180
- Yeah, and it really became clear to me

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:46.760
how maps are such a powerful
tool in demonstrating

00:26:46.760 --> 00:26:48.790
so many layers of disparity

00:26:48.790 --> 00:26:51.910
which is why I partnered with UICs,

00:26:53.470 --> 00:26:58.470
GIS department to create
an interactive mapping tool

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:04.670
on Folded Maps website where
you can layer the schools

00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:11.720
that are quality, the
businesses that are different

00:27:12.100 --> 00:27:14.430
on the North and South sides

00:27:14.430 --> 00:27:17.730
because it becomes even more clear

00:27:17.730 --> 00:27:20.920
when you start to add
those specific layers

00:27:20.920 --> 00:27:24.130
so I really appreciate the map you made

00:27:24.130 --> 00:27:28.050
about the foreclosures because
a lot of people don't know

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:32.403
how they disproportionately
affect black neighborhoods.

