00:30:16.540 --> 00:30:18.670
perhaps, it's disturbing a thing

00:30:18.670 --> 00:30:21.120
but on the premise that
the residents there

00:30:21.120 --> 00:30:25.940
would not have the money
to hire real estate experts

00:30:25.940 --> 00:30:29.100
and attorneys to go and challenge.

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:30.437
(indistinct)

00:30:30.437 --> 00:30:35.370
- It's so many ways in which
it's kind of discriminatory.

00:30:35.370 --> 00:30:37.810
What's happening now
specifically in Chicago

00:30:37.810 --> 00:30:42.033
is that a lot of homeowners of color,

00:30:42.940 --> 00:30:45.220
people are buying, are paying their taxes,

00:30:45.220 --> 00:30:46.540
ultimately buying their taxes

00:30:46.540 --> 00:30:50.980
and they have to not only fight to recover

00:30:50.980 --> 00:30:55.030
that but they have to stay
on top of paying their taxes

00:30:55.030 --> 00:30:58.130
because people just predatory,

00:30:58.130 --> 00:31:01.080
they wait for people to just be late

00:31:01.080 --> 00:31:05.470
and so Black and Brown
neighborhoods are subject to that.

00:31:05.470 --> 00:31:08.660
- Yap, there is a question
from one of the participants.

00:31:08.660 --> 00:31:11.290
Perhaps Bill, could you
show the Chicago map again?

00:31:11.290 --> 00:31:13.033
There might be a question forthcoming.

00:31:14.220 --> 00:31:16.660
And then in the meantime,
while he's pulling that up,

00:31:16.660 --> 00:31:19.633
what artists activists do you admire?

00:31:20.510 --> 00:31:23.933
- Oh my gosh, so many.

00:31:24.859 --> 00:31:26.500
(chuckles)

00:31:26.500 --> 00:31:29.273
Their names escape me right now.

00:31:31.400 --> 00:31:36.400
I can't remember her name but
the artists who did the edits

00:31:38.760 --> 00:31:42.870
of the New York Times articles, Alexandria

00:31:42.870 --> 00:31:47.870
I think it's Bale and we
pasted them across the city

00:31:48.400 --> 00:31:52.420
showing the difference between how stories

00:31:52.420 --> 00:31:56.250
that have black subjects are covered

00:31:56.250 --> 00:31:57.940
and worded very differently

00:31:57.940 --> 00:31:59.710
than stories that have white subjects,

00:31:59.710 --> 00:32:03.320
specifically crime related stories.

00:32:03.320 --> 00:32:08.320
And she did the edits and
the rewrites and showed how,

00:32:08.950 --> 00:32:11.857
and blocked out certain words
so that people could see

00:32:11.857 --> 00:32:13.960
very much like Folded Map.

00:32:13.960 --> 00:32:18.960
The actual difference in how
the subjects were described.

00:32:19.900 --> 00:32:23.170
And so, she's one that
I have been following

00:32:23.170 --> 00:32:26.283
and in amazement for a couple of years.

00:32:27.400 --> 00:32:29.610
There is an artist in New Orleans,

00:32:29.610 --> 00:32:33.320
her name is Shana Griffin.

00:32:33.320 --> 00:32:36.170
She has a project called "Displaced"

00:32:36.170 --> 00:32:39.440
and it focuses on utilizing maps

00:32:39.440 --> 00:32:44.410
but showing the economic
centers of New Orleans

00:32:44.410 --> 00:32:48.600
and how they are connected
to where slaves were sold.

00:32:48.600 --> 00:32:51.240
And so, that was fascinating to me

00:32:51.240 --> 00:32:54.850
to see the direct correlation
between the financial centers

00:32:54.850 --> 00:32:57.070
in New Orleans and how it's connects

00:32:57.070 --> 00:33:02.070
to the history of slavery
and selling slaves

00:33:03.170 --> 00:33:04.840
and how those exact locations

00:33:04.840 --> 00:33:08.700
became the financial
districts of New Orleans.

00:33:08.700 --> 00:33:13.320
So, that's an amazing project
that I have been noting out

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:17.010
for a long time now and
using as inspiration.

00:33:17.010 --> 00:33:18.470
But I have to think about the other ones

00:33:18.470 --> 00:33:20.523
'cause so many names come to mind.

00:33:22.500 --> 00:33:24.200
Thank you for the question though.

00:33:25.630 --> 00:33:26.760
- There was a request to
see that map again here.

00:33:26.760 --> 00:33:29.540
I'm just gonna share it briefly

00:33:29.540 --> 00:33:32.040
but I'll stop sharing so
you can see Tonika again.

00:33:35.320 --> 00:33:36.313
- [Tonika] Amazing.

00:33:38.690 --> 00:33:40.910
- So Bill, I had a
question about that map.

00:33:40.910 --> 00:33:43.050
Was all that data
publicly available to you

00:33:43.050 --> 00:33:44.173
or was it a bit of-

00:33:45.450 --> 00:33:47.050
- Oh yeah, its all public?

00:33:47.050 --> 00:33:51.090
So the census data you
can get from the web

00:33:51.090 --> 00:33:54.570
at the time I got it from
the Census Bureau website

00:33:54.570 --> 00:33:55.500
which is pretty clunky.

00:33:55.500 --> 00:33:59.780
Now it's much easier the
National Historical GIS, NHGIS.

00:33:59.780 --> 00:34:01.990
I'll put this in the chat as well.

00:34:01.990 --> 00:34:03.040
nhgis.org

00:34:04.588 --> 00:34:06.510
So, it's really easy to get census data

00:34:06.510 --> 00:34:11.110
and then I got a bunch of
data from the City of Chicago

00:34:11.110 --> 00:34:14.250
as well about parks and cemeteries

00:34:14.250 --> 00:34:15.600
and those kinds of things.

00:34:15.600 --> 00:34:17.200
And then the real work of the map

00:34:17.200 --> 00:34:20.530
was just really figuring
out where people didn't live

00:34:20.530 --> 00:34:23.670
'cause if you just plot all the
data in kind of big buckets,

00:34:23.670 --> 00:34:26.950
the dots spread out everywhere
but once you start figuring

00:34:26.950 --> 00:34:28.630
out where people don't live,

00:34:28.630 --> 00:34:29.990
where the industrial districts are

00:34:29.990 --> 00:34:32.370
and the shipping and the cemeteries

00:34:32.370 --> 00:34:33.210
and those kinds of things,

00:34:33.210 --> 00:34:36.590
then you really start to
see the real separations

00:34:36.590 --> 00:34:39.390
between the neighborhoods
really pop into place.

00:34:39.390 --> 00:34:41.430
But yeah, I know all the data's free

00:34:41.430 --> 00:34:44.680
and I found the web and I think
it really underscored for me

00:34:44.680 --> 00:34:49.230
how getting a totally
different sense of the city

00:34:49.230 --> 00:34:52.010
isn't necessarily always
about better data.

00:34:52.010 --> 00:34:53.660
It's about taking the data you have

00:34:53.660 --> 00:34:55.010
and doing something
really different with it

00:34:55.010 --> 00:34:57.020
using dots instead of solid dots.

00:34:57.020 --> 00:35:00.050
- Right, I'm reading off the chat.

00:35:00.050 --> 00:35:01.750
Somebody had a question that inspired me

00:35:01.750 --> 00:35:02.870
to ask this question.

00:35:02.870 --> 00:35:04.890
Wouldn't it be really revealing

00:35:04.890 --> 00:35:08.440
to overlay on top of this COVID cases

00:35:08.440 --> 00:35:10.550
and COVID fatalities, to layer on.

00:35:10.550 --> 00:35:11.890
So, Tonika is this something

00:35:11.890 --> 00:35:13.640
that you're thinking about right now?

00:35:13.640 --> 00:35:16.790
- Actually very early in the pandemic,

00:35:16.790 --> 00:35:20.370
I anticipated that disparity

00:35:20.370 --> 00:35:24.530
and I transformed my Instagram page

00:35:24.530 --> 00:35:28.130
from being my personal
account to my platform

00:35:28.130 --> 00:35:30.860
to share all of this
information in the pandemic

00:35:30.860 --> 00:35:34.000
and so I did have Instagram posts,

00:35:34.000 --> 00:35:37.290
I'm not a cartographer in the
sense that I make the maps

00:35:37.290 --> 00:35:40.960
but that was a dream of mine to overlay

00:35:40.960 --> 00:35:43.750
because I did have a set of three posts

00:35:43.750 --> 00:35:48.750
that showed Chicago segregation
in the fifties, sixties,

00:35:49.600 --> 00:35:52.530
and then the Chicago school closures

00:35:52.530 --> 00:35:53.900
that were also different

00:35:53.900 --> 00:35:58.900
and then ultimately the
Chicago map of the COVID cases.

00:35:59.296 --> 00:36:04.296
And it's very clear and
evident the disparity

00:36:06.560 --> 00:36:09.810
but I did that probably in May.

00:36:09.810 --> 00:36:13.360
I showed all three of those maps together

00:36:13.360 --> 00:36:18.340
so people could see consistently
where the disinvestment

00:36:18.340 --> 00:36:20.190
and disparity was really showing

00:36:20.190 --> 00:36:22.023
or having a negative impact.

00:36:23.030 --> 00:36:24.570
- I'm gonna share, again,

00:36:24.570 --> 00:36:27.650
this is from a page from a PDF

00:36:27.650 --> 00:36:31.120
made by the one of the local
unions here in New Haven.

00:36:31.120 --> 00:36:34.550
So, you'll see my map of
foreclosures in the upper left

00:36:34.550 --> 00:36:36.430
and then the map of
COVID in the lower right,

00:36:36.430 --> 00:36:39.400
and it's the same pattern.

00:36:39.400 --> 00:36:40.800
It's really, really amazing.

00:36:41.810 --> 00:36:44.030
And just putting those side by side

00:36:44.030 --> 00:36:45.610
with the same kinds of graphics,

00:36:45.610 --> 00:36:48.060
I think makes that point
really, really powerful.

00:36:49.219 --> 00:36:51.073
But the COVID map is not mine.

00:36:55.236 --> 00:36:56.280
- And what I have also been
to, I was just sharing the link

00:36:59.980 --> 00:37:02.530
to the two artists that
I've mentioned in the chat.

00:37:04.180 --> 00:37:05.720
What I've been thinking about lately

00:37:05.720 --> 00:37:10.610
especially if you will,
the reckoning of 2020,

00:37:10.610 --> 00:37:15.610
the fact that people are using
are saying systemic racism,

00:37:17.520 --> 00:37:20.330
are talking about
segregation and red lining

00:37:20.330 --> 00:37:23.130
in more public mainstream media

00:37:23.130 --> 00:37:26.663
and that has never happened before.

00:37:28.134 --> 00:37:31.390
I have been encouraging
people to understand

00:37:31.390 --> 00:37:36.390
that listening and really
recognizing the impact

00:37:38.525 --> 00:37:43.140
of all of these issues on black lives

00:37:43.140 --> 00:37:48.140
will afford our country the
opportunity to understand

00:37:48.350 --> 00:37:51.890
how all of the sectors,

00:37:51.890 --> 00:37:55.610
all of the sectors in
our country are impacted

00:37:55.610 --> 00:37:59.360
by racism and ultimately capitalism.

00:37:59.360 --> 00:38:04.360
But those go hand in hand and
it's the lives and experiences

00:38:05.680 --> 00:38:10.160
of black people that can
really show that connectivity,

00:38:10.160 --> 00:38:14.330
how it is in each of those
industries and sectors

00:38:14.330 --> 00:38:19.330
and so there's value beyond
just having a conscience

00:38:19.460 --> 00:38:21.790
and valuing black lives.

00:38:21.790 --> 00:38:25.240
But if we are interested in working

00:38:25.240 --> 00:38:30.240
towards a more fair country,
more equitable country,

00:38:31.370 --> 00:38:35.900
you can do that through the
recognition and understanding

00:38:35.900 --> 00:38:40.350
of how all of those
things impact black lives.

00:38:40.350 --> 00:38:44.800
- And I think the visual
argument is so much more powerful

00:38:44.800 --> 00:38:46.510
than even the verbal.

00:38:46.510 --> 00:38:48.770
- Yeah.
- It is just so stark

00:38:48.770 --> 00:38:52.360
and so visible and compelling, right.

00:38:52.360 --> 00:38:54.390
There's a way in which it is built.

00:38:54.390 --> 00:38:57.823
Wouldn't you say that is
one of the power of maps.

00:39:01.500 --> 00:39:03.010
- Oh, he has to get unmuted.

00:39:03.010 --> 00:39:03.843
I know

00:39:03.843 --> 00:39:04.950
(laughs)

00:39:04.950 --> 00:39:07.070
you have to wait for that.

00:39:07.070 --> 00:39:08.844
- That's right, that's right.

00:39:08.844 --> 00:39:11.860
But for me thinking back
to when I made that map

00:39:11.860 --> 00:39:13.520
for the first time,

00:39:13.520 --> 00:39:16.430
I think a lot of the
especially growing up,

00:39:16.430 --> 00:39:18.730
the South side was an idea.

00:39:18.730 --> 00:39:22.900
It was a nebulous place
that I didn't even know

00:39:22.900 --> 00:39:25.251
quite where it was or how big it was.

00:39:25.251 --> 00:39:27.640
And what I found making the map

00:39:27.640 --> 00:39:30.600
was that it really became
much more of a real place.

00:39:30.600 --> 00:39:32.980
And I start to see lots
of other places too

00:39:32.980 --> 00:39:34.330
that I'd never heard about.

00:39:35.205 --> 00:39:38.770
So, like you Tonika, I didn't
know much about the West side.

00:39:38.770 --> 00:39:41.510
The South side was the sort of the thing

00:39:41.510 --> 00:39:43.140
that everybody talked about.

00:39:43.140 --> 00:39:46.730
And then there's also black
neighborhoods outside in Cicero,

00:39:46.730 --> 00:39:47.680
I never even knew

00:39:47.680 --> 00:39:49.940
that there was a black
neighborhood in Cicero.

00:39:49.940 --> 00:39:52.980
And so maps kind of have
a strong visual argument

00:39:52.980 --> 00:39:53.910
that shows the segregation,

00:39:53.910 --> 00:39:57.690
they also make it really
specific and they make it real.

00:39:57.690 --> 00:40:01.020
They kind of anchor it in actual places

00:40:01.020 --> 00:40:06.020
in a way that I think did
much more powerful work for me

00:40:06.790 --> 00:40:08.620
than talking in coded language

00:40:08.620 --> 00:40:10.680
about the inner city
or even the South side

00:40:10.680 --> 00:40:13.343
which has its own kind of
coded language in Chicago.

00:40:14.360 --> 00:40:16.950
- Yes, when you talk about
geography in Chicago,

00:40:16.950 --> 00:40:18.499
you're talking about people.

00:40:18.499 --> 00:40:21.053
That's what it means in Chicago.

00:40:22.220 --> 00:40:23.930
- Further questions, the first,

00:40:23.930 --> 00:40:26.790
New Haven was split up by the highways,

00:40:26.790 --> 00:40:28.293
is this true in Chicago?

00:40:29.190 --> 00:40:30.164
- Yes,

00:40:30.164 --> 00:40:31.090
(chuckles)

00:40:31.090 --> 00:40:32.270
Yes.

00:40:32.270 --> 00:40:36.450
Just to answer directly without
tracing back the history,

00:40:36.450 --> 00:40:38.010
yes, indeed.

00:40:38.010 --> 00:40:39.510
Yes, indeed.

00:40:39.510 --> 00:40:41.570
- So, it's very deliberate as the point

00:40:41.570 --> 00:40:43.070
because highways were planned.

00:40:44.080 --> 00:40:45.530
They're very planned.

00:40:45.530 --> 00:40:49.177
I think that's what noting
the intentionality...

00:40:49.177 --> 00:40:51.927
(speaks faintly)

00:40:53.430 --> 00:40:55.730
- Second question from Professor Wechsler.

00:40:55.730 --> 00:40:56.990
I think it's really important to you.

00:40:56.990 --> 00:41:00.290
She says that there is a
generational aspect to this film.

00:41:00.290 --> 00:41:03.233
How are you thinking of
showing the history on the map?

00:41:05.100 --> 00:41:06.110
- Oh, interesting.

00:41:06.110 --> 00:41:11.110
Well, I'm first and foremost
starting with the personal

00:41:12.340 --> 00:41:17.340
because in order for the
history to even become valuable,

00:41:17.510 --> 00:41:21.320
people have to understand
the present day impact

00:41:21.320 --> 00:41:25.480
and that you can do intergenerationally.

00:41:25.480 --> 00:41:27.550
And starting with that,

00:41:27.550 --> 00:41:31.830
then the connection to
the history can be made.

00:41:31.830 --> 00:41:34.530
So, I am actually in the process

00:41:34.530 --> 00:41:38.450
of creating curricular
resources or using Folded Map

00:41:38.450 --> 00:41:43.120
as a curricular resources for
teachers in Social Science

00:41:43.120 --> 00:41:47.930
and in Art where the students can examine

00:41:47.930 --> 00:41:52.930
their own environment first
and then use that as an entree

00:41:53.410 --> 00:41:58.410
into being connected to the
historical forces decisions

00:41:59.010 --> 00:42:01.460
that have influenced
their present day life.

00:42:01.460 --> 00:42:04.830
So, that is the route I'm taking

00:42:04.830 --> 00:42:08.550
in developing the Folded Map curriculum

00:42:08.550 --> 00:42:10.410
and those are the ways in which teachers

00:42:10.410 --> 00:42:13.480
and educators have been using
it to help their students

00:42:13.480 --> 00:42:16.643
understand the history of segregation.

00:42:21.420 --> 00:42:22.540
I hope that hits

00:42:22.540 --> 00:42:24.090
- For the questions in the chat,

00:42:24.090 --> 00:42:25.743
Priya, could you have anything else?

00:42:26.650 --> 00:42:31.650
- No, I was just going to ask Tonika,

00:42:37.160 --> 00:42:40.170
was it photography that first got you,

00:42:40.170 --> 00:42:42.170
as you talked about in the film,

00:42:42.170 --> 00:42:44.110
that you sort of you understood

00:42:44.110 --> 00:42:45.590
what your trip to your school

00:42:45.590 --> 00:42:49.540
was about in terms of the
change in the landscape

00:42:49.540 --> 00:42:52.630
but I was curious about
how you got to photography

00:42:52.630 --> 00:42:54.750
as a medium as an artist.

00:42:54.750 --> 00:42:56.770
- Oh, yes and I'll answer that.

00:42:56.770 --> 00:43:01.060
I just saw Laura's clarification
to her question pop up.

00:43:01.060 --> 00:43:06.060
She was talking about inter
personal within the family.

00:43:07.510 --> 00:43:10.570
So, in regards to Folded Map

00:43:10.570 --> 00:43:12.180
and connecting it to family history,

00:43:12.180 --> 00:43:17.180
that's also a part of using
it as a curricular resource.

00:43:17.240 --> 00:43:20.250
Having students ask their parents,

00:43:20.250 --> 00:43:23.870
ask their their grandparents
the very same question

00:43:23.870 --> 00:43:27.210
that I pose to everyone,
how did you come to live

00:43:28.100 --> 00:43:30.250
either in the neighborhood or in the city?

00:43:30.250 --> 00:43:33.880
So, teachers have definitely
made that an assignment

00:43:33.880 --> 00:43:36.480
for students to ask their parents

00:43:36.480 --> 00:43:39.450
how did they come to
live in this neighborhood

00:43:39.450 --> 00:43:42.015
and then to also ask their grandparents.

00:43:42.015 --> 00:43:45.700
So, interviewing techniques
have definitely been applied.

00:43:45.700 --> 00:43:47.350
But Priya to go to your question,

00:43:47.350 --> 00:43:49.653
how I got introduced to photography.

00:43:52.270 --> 00:43:56.640
Really, my family
specifically my maternal side

00:43:58.020 --> 00:44:00.620
and my grandmother as
well she's an artist.

00:44:00.620 --> 00:44:05.020
And my mom is a writer, a poet,

00:44:05.020 --> 00:44:09.030
my uncles that I grew up with,
they're also visual artists.

00:44:09.030 --> 00:44:13.440
So, art was just valued
so much in my home.

00:44:13.440 --> 00:44:15.760
I often joke with people
that it was valued so much

00:44:15.760 --> 00:44:19.350
that when I decided that
I was interested in music,

00:44:19.350 --> 00:44:23.425
my grandmother and my
mom bought me a piano.

00:44:23.425 --> 00:44:24.499
(chuckles)

00:44:24.499 --> 00:44:27.540
And I didn't even stick
with piano for that long.

00:44:27.540 --> 00:44:30.210
Maybe like five, six
years but I had a piano.

00:44:30.210 --> 00:44:33.030
So, they really art

00:44:33.030 --> 00:44:38.030
and so when my mom and my dad were married

00:44:38.330 --> 00:44:40.500
and we were living together,

00:44:40.500 --> 00:44:44.550
my father had a fascination for cameras.

00:44:44.550 --> 00:44:47.700
He never evolved into being
a professional photographer

00:44:47.700 --> 00:44:52.080
but I grew up around a
camera in my face so much

00:44:52.080 --> 00:44:57.080
that it sparked a curiosity
in me very early on to use it.

00:44:57.450 --> 00:44:59.440
So, by the time I was in eighth grade,

00:44:59.440 --> 00:45:02.630
I was already kind of
fascinated with with cameras

00:45:02.630 --> 00:45:07.133
and then taking pictures,
so that's how it evolved.

00:45:08.530 --> 00:45:11.650
- Anyway, that sounds fabulous.

00:45:11.650 --> 00:45:13.130
But before I say, thank you.

00:45:13.130 --> 00:45:16.690
I just wanted to check in that
Richard and Barbara Franke

00:45:16.690 --> 00:45:18.650
are old residents of Chicago,

00:45:18.650 --> 00:45:23.380
so, Barbara, do you have
any particular question

00:45:23.380 --> 00:45:26.170
and Ty could you unmute Barbara please?

00:45:26.170 --> 00:45:28.570
- Yeah, I'm trying to unmute her now.

00:45:28.570 --> 00:45:29.590
There we go.

00:45:29.590 --> 00:45:33.840
- Yes. I'm very curious
it's fascinating to me

00:45:35.760 --> 00:45:40.710
but I'm also very curious
there is an exodus

00:45:40.710 --> 00:45:44.940
from the black community
to return to the South.

00:45:44.940 --> 00:45:48.020
Do you think some of the
questions that you have used

00:45:48.020 --> 00:45:53.020
Tonika, for your project would also relate

00:45:55.860 --> 00:45:57.203
to those who are leaving?

00:45:59.120 --> 00:46:00.943
- Yes, definitely.

00:46:01.830 --> 00:46:03.360
It's interesting that
you're bringing that up

00:46:03.360 --> 00:46:08.360
because as a 41 year old black
lifetime resident of Chicago,

00:46:09.650 --> 00:46:14.650
I definitely can refer to the
exoduses of black Chicago.

00:46:20.298 --> 00:46:24.400
15, 20 years ago, I definitely
remember my age group

00:46:24.400 --> 00:46:27.120
talking about moving to Atlanta.

00:46:27.120 --> 00:46:31.760
It was a huge exodus of
black Chicagoans my age

00:46:31.760 --> 00:46:34.410
at that time moving to Atlanta,

00:46:34.410 --> 00:46:39.410
then Texas, Austin, now North Carolina

00:46:39.690 --> 00:46:44.190
and it mostly dealt with
not only just home ownership

00:46:44.190 --> 00:46:47.850
or the lack of housing
stock in black neighborhoods

00:46:47.850 --> 00:46:52.730
and how overpriced houses
were in other neighborhoods

00:46:52.730 --> 00:46:56.100
that ended that ultimately
unpredominantly why in Chicago

00:46:56.100 --> 00:46:59.000
but the lack of amenities and resources

00:46:59.000 --> 00:47:02.530
and how difficult it is to live close

00:47:02.530 --> 00:47:04.910
to where you probably will be working

00:47:04.910 --> 00:47:08.150
and all of those decisions
to all of those elements

00:47:08.150 --> 00:47:11.960
as well as the continuation of schools

00:47:11.960 --> 00:47:15.800
in black neighborhoods
being closed and going down

00:47:15.800 --> 00:47:20.740
in quality as a result
of the population loss.

00:47:20.740 --> 00:47:25.740
All of that together made it
bleak for young black families

00:47:27.650 --> 00:47:32.210
in Chicago which up added
to them seeking opportunity

00:47:32.210 --> 00:47:35.240
in that reverse migration down South.

00:47:35.240 --> 00:47:39.920
They can take their Chicago
salaries, their education

00:47:39.920 --> 00:47:43.660
and just have a better quality
of life and get a larger home

00:47:43.660 --> 00:47:45.850
in some of those Southern
cities and States,

00:47:45.850 --> 00:47:48.493
and it's still kind of happening now.

